{{Nowrap|[[Right-wing politics|Right-wing]]|ref|{{cite book |author=Uri Ram |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Changing_Agenda_of_Israeli_Sociology/BVF6XquvES8C?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22right-wing%22+%22Herut%22&pg=PA12 |title=The Changing Agenda of Israeli Sociology: Theory, Ideology, and Identity |quote=The aftermath of the Six Day War also gave a new impetus to the old (and thought to be obsolete) nationalistic right-wing Herut party, the successor of the Yishuv's revisionist movement, which demanded Israeli possession of "the entire land of Israel". |publisher=[[State University of New York Press]] |date=February 2012 |page=12 }}|</ref>| to [[Far-right politics in Israel|far-right]]|refn|ref|{{cite book |editor1=Eric C. Browne |editor2=John Dreijmanis |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Government_Coalitions_in_Western_Democra/bxoNAQAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=%22far-right%22+%22Herut%22&dq=%22far-right%22+%22Herut%22 |title=Government Coalitions in Western Democracies |quote=Grouped now in the parliamentary bloc Likud, they are led by Herut at the far right of the political scale. |publisher=[[Longman]] |date=1982 |page=292 }}|</ref>|ref|{{cite book |title=Legislative and Special Analyses: Issue 18 |quote=... Herut (Freedom), at the far right of the Israeli political spectrum, was founded in 1948 by Menahem Begin, leader of the Irgun Zvai Leumi terrorist organization. |publisher=University of Michigan |date=1970 |page=97 }}|</ref>|ref|{{cite book |author=American University (Washington, D.C.). Foreign Area Studies |editor=Richard F. Nyrop |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Israel_a_Country_Study/-KFZAbLeGIwC?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=%22far-right%22+%22Herut%22&dq=%22far-right%22+%22Herut%22 |title=
