value:
{{Nowrap|[[Centrism|Centre]]|refn|ref| name="Cambridge University Press"|{{Citation |chapter=Allying with Militants? the Pakistan Muslim League-Nawaz and Sectarian Groups in Punjab |date=2022 |title=Under the Gun: Political Parties and Violence in Pakistan |pages=140–172 |editor-last=Siddiqui |editor-first=Niloufer A. |chapter-url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/under-the-gun/allying-with-militants-the-pakistan-muslim-leaguenawaz-and-sectarian-groups-in-punjab/F593A1224C9D8847BBCA206BC0C05483 |access-date=2026-06-04 |place=Cambridge |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=978-1-009-24253-0 |quote=PML-N is not sectarian nor ideological; rather, it is centrist, organizationally weak, and patronage-driven}}|</ref>|ref|{{Cite web |last=Chughtai |first=Alia |last2=Hussain |first2=Abid |title=Pakistan election 2024: Which are the major political parties? |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/2/3/pakistan-elections-2024-here-are-the-major-political-parties |access-date=2026-06-04 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en |quote=The Pakistan Muslim League-Nawaz, a centrist party led by former Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif, came to power for the third time in 2013 with a clear majority}}|</ref>|ref| name="france24.com"|{{Cite web |date=2022-04-11 |title=Pakistani MPs elect Shahbaz Sharif as new prime minister after Khan ouster |url=https://www.france24.com/en/asia-pacific/20220411-pakistani-mps-elect-shahbaz-sharif-as-new-prime-minister-after-khan-ouster |access-date=2026-06-04 |website=France 24 |language=en |quote=Sharif, leader of the centrist Pakistan Muslim League-N (PML-N) was the only candidate after Khan loyalist Shah Mahmood Qureshi, the former foreign minister, withdrew his candidacy and resigned his seat}}|</ref>|ref|{{cite web |title=Pakistan’s Shehbaz Sharif submits nomination to become new PM |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/10/pakistans-shehbaz-sharif-submits-nomination-to-become-new-pm |website=Al Jazeera |language=en |quote=Shehbaz, the centrist Pakistan Muslim League-N (PML-N) party chief, is widely expected to be named the country’s new prime minister on Monday. |access-date=23 June 2026 |archive-date=3 January 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260103165828/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/10/pakistans-shehbaz-sharif-submits-nomination-to-become-new-pm |url-status=live }}|</ref>|ref|{{cite web |last1=Paracha |first1=Nadeem F. |title=SMOKERS’ CORNER: THE VICIOUS CYCLES OF PAKISTAN'S POLITICS |url=https://www.dawn.com/news/1581596 |website=Dawn |language=en |date=27 September 2020 |quote=The PDM, led by the centrist PML-N and liberal-left PPP, has accused the centre-right PTI government of working with the military-establishment to form a ‘hybrid democracy’, dominated by the latter, with the former becoming its civilian vassal to erode political opposition against this arrangement.}}|</ref>| to [[Centre-right politics|centre-right]]|refn|ref|{{cite news |title=Sharif declares victory for his party in Pakistan vote |newspaper=Hindustan Times |date=12 May 2013
|url=http://www.hindustantimes.com/india/sharif-declares-victory-for-his-party-in-pakistan-vote/story-E9DADY2PUh57OCK5g3Fg2J.html |url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170812095306/http://www.hindustantimes.com/india/sharif-declares-victory-for-his-party-in-pakistan-vote/story-E9DADY2PUh57OCK5g3Fg2J.html |archive-date=12 August 2017 |df=dmy-all|quote=Former prime minister Nawaz Sharif late Saturday declared victory for his centre-right opposition Pakistan Muslim League-N (PML-N) party and invited other parties to work with him.}}|</ref>|ref|{{citation |title=Nawaz Sharif declares his party victorious in Pakistan vote |date=11 May 2013 |publisher=Al Arabiya |url=http://english.alarabiya.net/en/News/asia/2013/05/11/Nawaz-Sharif-declares-victory-in-Pakistan-election-.html |url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130512081625/http://english.alarabiya.net/en/News/asia/2013/05/11/Nawaz-Sharif-declares-victory-in-Pakistan-election-.html |archive-date=12 May 2013 |df=dmy-all|quote=Flanked by his close family, Sharif gave a victory speech to hundreds of jubilant supporters who shouted “Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif” at his center-right Pakistan Muslim League-N party headquarters in Lahore.}}|</ref>|ref|{{citation |title=Nawaz Sharif Set for Third Term as PM |newspaper=India Times |date=12 May 2013 |url=http://www.indiatimes.com/news/asia/nawaz-sharif-set-for-third-term-as-pm-77130.html |url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130512184044/http://www.indiatimes.com/news/asia/nawaz-sharif-set-for-third-term-as-pm-77130.html |archive-date=12 May 2013 |df=dmy-all|quote=Sharif's centre-right Pakistan Muslim League-N (PML-N) was leading the race with 126 seats and Khan's Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf (PTI) had 34. Pakistan People's Party with just 32 seats so far had a poor showing in the polls, Geo TV reported.}}|</ref>|ref|{{cite book|editor=Vineeta Yadav |title=Religious Parties and the Politics of Civil Liberties |url=https://b