{{Tree list}} * [[Iron Guard#Ideology|Legionarism]] {{refn|group|=|n|The [[clerical fascist]]|ref|Iordachi, Constantin (2023). ''[https://www.routledge.com/The-Fascist-Faith-of-the-Legion-Archangel-Michael-in-Romania-192741/Iordachi/p/book/9781138624559 The Fascist Faith of the Legion "Archangel Michael" in Romania, 1927–41 Martyrdom and National Purification] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230213090854/https://www.routledge.com/The-Fascist-Faith-of-the-Legion-Archangel-Michael-in-Romania-192741/Iordachi/p/book/9781138624559 |date=2023-02-13}}''. [[Routledge]]. {{ISBN|978-1138624559}}.|</ref>|ref| name="fascism"|{{cite web |title=fascism |url=https://www.britannica.com/topic/fascism |publisher=Encyclopædia Britannica |access-date=2025-04-20}}|</ref>| Iron Guard synthesized [[Orthodoxy|Orthodox christianity]] with [[revolutionary nationalism]] and were heavily inspired by the writings of [[Nichifor Crainic]].|ref| name="clark"|{{cite journal |last=Clark |first=Roland |title=Nationalism and orthodoxy: Nichifor Crainic and the political culture of the extreme right in 1930s Romania |journal=Nationalities Papers |publisher=Cambridge University Press (CUP) |volume=40 |issue=1 |year=2012 |issn=0090-5992 |doi=10.1080/00905992.2011.633076 |pages=107–126 |s2cid=153813255 |quote=The institute only lasted one year, but allowed Crainic to advance ideas such as anti-Masonry, anti-Semitism, and biological racism within an LANC-approved forum (Crainic, ''Ortodoxie'' 147).}}|</ref>|ref|{{cite journal | issn=1231-1413 |first=Ovidiu |last=Caraiani |journal=Polish Sociological Review |number=142 |pages=161–169 |publisher=Polskie Towarzystwo Socjologiczne (Polish Sociological Association) |title=Identities and Rights in Romanian Political Discourse |quote=Nae Ionescu considered ethnicity as "the formula of today's Romanian nationalism," while for Nichifor Crainic the "biological homogeneousness," the "historical identity" and the "blood and the soil" were the defining elements of the "ethnocratic state."|jstor=41274855 |year=2003}}|</ref>|ref|{{cite journal | issn=0028-8683 |first=Michael |last=Wedekind |journal=New Zealand Slavonic Journal |pages=27–67 |publisher=Australia and New Zealand Slavists’ Association |title=The mathematization of the human being: anthropology and ethno-politics in Romania during the late 1930s and early 1940s |volume=44 |year=2010 |quote=A prominent proponent of the concept of 'ethnic homogeneity' was the chauvinistic, xenophobic and pro-Nazi writer, politician, poet and professor of Theology Nichifor Crainic (1889–1972), author of "Orthodoxy and Ethnocracy" (''Ortodoxie și etnocrație''), published in 1938.|jstor=41759355}}|</ref>}} ** [[Ultranationalism]] Iron Guard synthesized [[Orthodoxy|Orthodox christianity]] with [[revolutionary nationalism]] and were heavily inspired by the writings of [[Nichifor Crainic]]. {{refn|group|=|n|The Iron Guard articulated a distinctive variant of [[Romanian nationalism#Fascist period and World War II|ultranationalism]] that integrated [[Religious nationalism|transcendental and explicitly religious dimensions]].|ref| name="britannica.com" | This ideological framework, often characterized as "thanatic nationalism", manifested itself through ritualized political funerals, a cultivated veneration of the dead, and [[soteriological]] and martyrological motifs drawn from Orthodox Christian tradition. At its core was a redemptive political theology grounded in the principle of "self-sacrificial patriotism". By employing funerary rites and commemorative ceremonies, the movement constructed a symbolic pantheon of political martyrs and advanced the Fascist ideal of the "[[New Man (utopian concept)#Fascism|new man]]" (|langx|ro|omul nou|); in this case, an individual who sought spiritual "purification" as a means of bringing the entire nation into closer alignment with [[God in Christianity|God]].|refn|ref|{{Citation |last=Rusu |first=Mihai S. |title=The Sacralization of Martyric Death in Romanian Legionary Movement: Self-sacrificial Patriotism, Vicarious Atonement, and Thanatic Nationalism |date=2016 |journal=Politics, Religion & Ideology |volume=17 |issue=2–3 |pages=249–273 |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/21567689.2016.1232196 |access-date=2023-09-13 |language=en |doi=10.1080/21567689.2016.1232196|url-access=subscription }}|</ref>|ref|{{Citation |last=Rusu |first=Mihai S. |title=Staging Death: Christofascist Necropolitics during the National Legionary State in Romania, 1940–1941 |date=2021 |journal=Nationalities Papers |volume=49 |issue=3 |pages=576–589 |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/nationalities-papers/article/abs/staging-death-christofascist-necropolitics-during-the-national-legionary-state-in-romania-19401941/F44F3485CB3F6E00602007710A1D4BDA |access-date=2023-09-13 |language=en |doi=10.1017/nps.2020.22|url-access=subscription}}|</ref>|ref| name="veiga"|[[Francisco Veiga]], ''Istoria Gărzii de Fier, 1919–1941: Mistica ultranaţionalismului'' ("History of the Iron Guard, 1919–1941: The Mistique of Ultra-Nationalism"), Bucharest, [[Humanitas publishing house|Humanitas]], 1993 (Romanian-language version of the 1989 Spanish edition ''La mística del ultranacionalismo (Historia de la Guardia de Hierro) Rumania, 1919–1941'', Bellaterra, Publicacions de la [[Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona]], {{ISBN|84-7488-497-7}}), p.47–49, 224–226, 263, 285–286, 292–293, 301|</ref>|ref|[[Stanley G. Payne]], ''Fascism'', [[University of Wisconsin Press]], Madison, 1980. p. 116. {{ISBN|0-299-08064-1}}.|</ref>|ref|{{cite book|title=History of the Holocaust – Romania |last=Ancel|first=Jean|year=2002|publisher=[[Yad Vashem]]|location=[[Israel]]|isbn=965-308-157-8|pages=354–61|language=he}}|</ref>|ref| name="Zelinka 2009 p. 42"|{{cite book |last=Zelinka |first=Elisabeta |chapter=Xenophobia, anti-Semitism and feminist activism in eastern Europe: a case study of Romania |chapter-url=https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctt5vjc6k.7 |chapter-url-access=subscription |editor-last=Huggan |editor-first=Graham |editor2-last=Law |editor2-first=Ian |title=Racism postcolonialism Europe |series=Postcolonialism Across the Disciplines |volume=6 |publisher=Liverpool University Press |publication-place=Liverpool |year=2009 |isbn=978-1-84631-562-6 |oclc=865564960 |page=[{{GBurl |id=7G5vEAAAQBAJ |pg=PA42}} 42] |doi=10.2307/j.ctt5vjc6k.7 |quote=The Iron Guard was the ultra-nationalist, anti-Semitic, fascist movement and political party in Romania. |access-date=2024-03-03 |archive-date=2024-03-05 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240305045423/https://chooser.crossref.org/?doi=10.2307%2Fj.ctt5vjc6k.7 |url-status=live}}|</ref>|ref| name="Payne 2017"|{{cite web |last=Payne |first=Stanley G. |title=Why Romania's Fascist Movement Was Unusually Morbid – Even for Fascists |website=Slate Magazine |date=2017-02-21 |url=https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2017/02/romanias-unusually-morbid-fascist-movement-blended-nationalistic-violence-with-fanatical-christian-martyrdom.html |access-date=2024-03-03 |quote=''A Unique Death Cult'': How the Romanian Iron Guard blended nationalistic violence with Christian martyrdom to spread a singularly morbid fascist movement. [...] As in some other Eastern European countries, there had developed strong currents of ''populism'' that espoused a kind of peasant nationalism, equally opposed to liberalism, conservatism, and Marxist socialism. |archive-date=2023-12-11 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231211064438/https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2017/02/romanias-unusually-morbid-fascist-movement-blended-nationalistic-violence-with-fanatical-christian-martyrdom.html |url-status=live}}|</ref>}} ** [[Populism]] This ideological framework, often characterized as "thanatic nationalism", manifested itself through ritualized political funerals, a cultivated veneration of the dead, and [[soteriological]] and martyrological motifs drawn from Orthodox Christian tradition. At its core was a redemptive political theology grounded in the principle of "self-sacrificial patriotism". By employing funerary rites and commemorative ceremonies, the movement constructed a symbolic pantheon of political martyrs and advanced the Fascist ideal of the "[[New Man (utopian concept)#Fascism|new man]]" ( {{langx|ro|omul nou}} ); in this case, an individual who sought spiritual "purification" as a means of bringing the entire nation into closer alignment with [[God in Christianity|God]]. {{refn|ref|{{Citation |last=Rusu |first=Mihai S. |title=The Sacralization of Martyric Death in Romanian Legionary Movement: Self-sacrificial Patriotism, Vicarious Atonement, and Thanatic Nationalism |date=2016 |journal=Politics, Religion & Ideology |volume=17 |issue=2–3 |pages=249–273 |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/21567689.2016.1232196 |access-date=2023-09-13 |language=en |doi=10.1080/21567689.2016.1232196|url-access=subscription }}|</ref>|ref|{{Citation |last=Rusu |first=Mihai S. |title=Staging Death: Christofascist Necropolitics during the National Legionary State in Romania, 1940–1941 |date=2021 |journal=Nationalities Papers |volume=49 |issue=3 |pages=576–589 |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/nationalities-papers/article/abs/staging-death-christofascist-necropolitics-during-the-national-legionary-state-in-romania-19401941/F44F3485CB3F6E00602007710A1D4BDA |access-date=2023-09-13 |language=en |doi=10.1017/nps.2020.22|url-access=subscription}}|</ref>|ref| name="veiga"|[[Francisco Veiga]], ''Istoria Gărzii de Fier, 1919–1941: Mistica ultranaţionalismului'' ("History of the Iron Guard, 1919–1941: The Mistique of Ultra-Nationalism"), Bucharest, [[Humanitas publishing house|Humanitas]], 1993 (Romanian-language version of the 1989 Spanish edition ''La mística del ultranacionalismo (Historia de la Guardia de Hierro) Rumania, 1919–1941'', Bellaterra, Publicacions de la [[Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona]], {{ISBN|84-7488-497-7}}), p.47–49, 224–226, 263, 285–286, 292–293, 301|</ref>|ref|[[Stanley G. Payne]], ''Fascism'', [[University of Wisconsin Press]], Madison, 1980. p. 116. {{ISBN|0-299-08064-1}}.|</ref>|ref|{{cite book|title=History of the Holocaust – Romania |last=Ancel|first=Jean|year=2002|publisher=[[Yad Vashem]]|location=[[Israel]]|isbn=965-308-157-8|pages=354–61|language=he}}|</ref>|ref| name="Zelinka 2009 p. 42"|{{cite book |last=Zelinka |first=Elisabeta |chapter=Xenophobia, anti-Semitism and feminist activism in eastern Europe: a case study of Romania |chapter-url=https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctt5vjc6k.7 |chapter-url-access=subscription |editor-last=Huggan |editor-first=Graham |editor2-last=Law |editor2-first=Ian |title=Racism postcolonialism Europe |series=Postcolonialism Across the Disciplines |volume=6 |publisher=Liverpool University Press |publication-place=Liverpool |year=2009 |isbn=978-1-84631-562-6 |oclc=865564960 |page=[{{GBurl |id=7G5vEAAAQBAJ |pg=PA42}} 42] |doi=10.2307/j.ctt5vjc6k.7 |quote=The Iron Guard was the ultra-nationalist, anti-Semitic, fascist movement and political party in Romania. |access-date=2024-03-03 |archive-date=2024-03-05 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240305045423/https://chooser.crossref.org/?doi=10.2307%2Fj.ctt5vjc6k.7 |url-status=live}}|</ref>|ref| name="Payne 2017"|{{cite web |last=Payne |first=Stanley G. |title=Why Romania's Fascist Movement Was Unusually Morbid – Even for Fascists |website=Slate Magazine |date=2017-02-21 |url=https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2017/02/romanias-unusually-morbid-fascist-movement-blended-nationalistic-violence-with-fanatical-christian-martyrdom.html |access-date=2024-03-03 |quote=''A Unique Death Cult'': How the Romanian Iron Guard blended nationalistic violence with Christian martyrdom to spread a singularly morbid fascist movement. [...] As in some other Eastern European countries, there had developed strong currents of ''populism'' that espoused a kind of peasant nationalism, equally opposed to liberalism, conservatism, and Marxist socialism. |archive-date=2023-12-11 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231211064438/https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2017/02/romanias-unusually-morbid-fascist-movement-blended-nationalistic-violence-with-fanatical-christian-martyrdom.html |url-status=live}}|</ref>}} {{refn|ref| name="Historia 2018-Tăpălagă 2020-Iordachi 200–Roberts Glăjar 2022-Teodorescu 2022 pp. 65–80"|* {{cite web |title=Rădăcinile intelectuale ale legionarismului |trans-title=The intellectual roots of legionaryism |website=Historia |date=2018-08-28 |url=https://historia.ro/sectiune/general/radacinile-intelectuale-ale-legionarismului-570000.html |language=ro |access-date=2024-03-03 |archive-date=2023-12-04 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204093124/https://historia.ro/sectiune/general/radacinile-intelectuale-ale-legionarismului-570000.html |url-status=live}}
* {{cite web |last=Tăpălagă |first=Dan |title=AUR și Mișcarea Legionară. Cât de valabilă este comparația între cele două partide |trans-title=AUR and the Legionary Movement. How valid is the comparison between the two parties |website=G4Media.ro |date=2020-12-21 |url=https://www.g4media.ro/aur-si-miscarea-legionara-cat-de-valabila-este-comparatia-intre-cele-doua-partide.html |language=ro |access-date=2024-03-03 |archive-date=2021-10-19 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211019100620/https://www.g4media.ro/aur-si-miscarea-legionara-cat-de-valabila-este-comparatia-intre-cele-doua-partide.html |url-status=live}}
* {{cite book |last=Iordachi |first=Constantin |chapter=Charisma, Religion, and Ideology: Romania’s Interwar Legion of the Archangel Michael |chapter-url=https://books.openedition.org/ceup/2418 |editor-last=Lampe |editor-first=John |editor-link=John R. Lampe |editor2-last=Mazower |editor2-first=Mark |editor2-link=Mark Mazower |title=Ideologies and National Identities |url=https://books.openedition.org/ceup/2396 |publisher=Central European University Press |year=2006 |isbn=978-963-9241-82-4 |oclc=945782879 |pages=19–53 |via=OpenEdition Books |access-date=2024-03-03 |archive-date=2024-03-03 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240303193124/https://books.openedition.org/ceup/2396 |url-status=live}}
* {{cite web |last1=Roberts |first1=Jason |last2=Glăjar |first2=Sergio |title=The Legionary Phenomenon: A Romanian Fascist Manifesto |website=illiberalism.org |date=2022-02-22 |url=https://www.illiberalism.org/the-legionary-phenomenon-a-romanian-fascist-manifesto |access-date=2024-03-03 |archive-date=2023-03-31 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230331025553/https://www.illiberalism.org/the-legionary-phenomenon-a-romanian-fascist-manifesto |url-status=live}}
* {{cite journal |last1=Teodorescu |first1=Bogdan |last2=Sultănescu |first2=Dan |title=Mişcarea Legionară, între istorie și percepții actuale. Amestec de fascism și atitudini populiste |trans-title=The Legionary Movement, between history and current perceptions. Mixture of fascism and populist attitudes |journal=Polis. Journal of Political Science |publisher=Editura Institutul European |volume=X |issue=3 (37) |date=2022 |issn=1221-9762 |pages=65–80 |url=https://www.ceeol.com/search/article-detail?id=1064277 |oclc=9627670651 |access-date=2024-03-03 |archive-date=2023-02-13 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230213090803/https://www.ceeol.com/search/article-detail?id=1064277 |url-status=live}}|</ref>}} ** [[Antisemitism]] {{refn|group|=|n|The Romanian Iron Guard espoused anti-capitalist, anti-banking and anti-bourgeois rhetoric, combined with anti-communism and a [[Antisemitism in Christianity|religious form of antisemitism]].|sfn|Mann|2004|pp|=|268–269|ref|{{cite book|author-last=Crampton |author-first=R.J. |title=Eastern Europe in the Twentieth Century |location=London & New York |publisher=[[Routledge]] |date=1994 |page=165}}|</ref>| The Iron Guard saw both capitalism and communism as being Jewish creations that served to divide the nation, and accused Jews of being "the enemies of the Christian nation".|sfn|Mann|2004|p|=|270}} {{sfn|Mann|2004|pp|=|268–269}} The Iron Guard saw both capitalism and communism as being Jewish creations that served to divide the nation, and accused Jews of being "the enemies of the Christian nation". {{sfn|Mann|2004|p|=|270}} {{Tree list/end}}

